#Problem APEX 2.2.17

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Differentiation)
## DBsection(Conceptual understanding of derivatives)
## KEYWORDS('graphs', 'derivative')
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## MLT(graphsderivatives)
## Level(3)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('2.2')
## Problem1('17')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'plots.pl', 'parserPopUp.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl');

$a = non_zero_random(-5, 5) / 2;
$b = random(-3, 3);

$plot = Plot(
    xmin            => -10,
    xmax            =>  10,
    xtick_delta     =>  5,
    xminor          =>  4,
    ymin            => -10,
    ymax            =>  10,
    ytick_delta     =>  5,
    yminor          =>  4,
    rounded_corners => 1
);
$plot->add_function(
    [
        "$a/(x-$b)", 'x', -10, 10,
        color     => 'blue',
        linestyle => 'dashed',
        width     => 1.5
    ],
    [ "-$a/(x-$b)^2", 'x', -10, 10, color => 'green', width => 1.5 ]
);

$altText =
    "A graph with two plots.  The blue graph is dashed and starts near "
    . "the negative x-axis and decreases to off the plot at x=$b, there is a jump to "
    . "the top of the plot and decreases smoothly to near the positive x-axis. "
    . "The green curve starts near the negative x-axis, decreases to off the plot "
    . "at x=$b, then nearby increases to the positive x-axis.";

$ans_a = "f is dashed blue and its derivative is green";
$ans_b = "f is green and its derivative is dashed blue";
$ans   = DropDown([ [ $ans_a, $ans_b ] ], $ans_b);

BEGIN_PGML
The graphs of two functions are shown below.  One is the graph
of [`f(x)`] and the other is the graph of its derivative,
[`f'(x)`].  Identify which graph is which.

>> [![$altText]!]{$plot}{300} <<

[_]{$ans}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Recall that the derivative is the slope of the function.

- When [`f(x)`] is increasing, then [`f'(x)>0`]
- When [`f(x)`] is decreasing, then [`f'(x)<0`]
- At the relative mins and max of [`f(x)`] are the [`x`]-intercepts of [`f'(x)`].

Therefore, [$ans_a].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
